            "explanation": "Step 1:   It needs to be recognised as conditional probability.  We need to know the total number of boys, and the number of boys who chose swimming.\nStep 2:  Total number of boys  =  120 – 46  =  74\nStep 3:    of 36  =  24, so 24 boys chose tennis\nStep 4:  Number of students who chose swimming  =  120 – 36 (tennis) – 27 (archery)  =  57\nStep 5:  Of these, 25 were girls, so the number of boys who chose swimming is  57 – 25  =  32\nSo, if a boy is chosen at random, the probability that he chose swimming is\nAlternatively, we can use a 2-way table\nswimming\narchery\ntennis\ntotal\nboy\nstep 5 (32)\n27-9 (= 18)\nstep 3 (24)\nstep 2 (74)\ngirl\n25\n46-12-25 (= 9)\n12\n46\nstep 4 (57)\n27\n36\n120\nleading to:\nswimming\narchery\ntennis\ntotal\nboy\n32\n18\n24\n74\ngirl\n25\n9\n12\n46\n57\n27\n36\n120\nthen, from the table, if a boy is chosen at random, the probability that he chose swimming is:",
